@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+{
+  "source": "assistant_text, verbatim, from real POST /agent/v1/turn responses on the OpenAI route at CEE staging 57f903c (AI Quality's route captures for the pricing and hiring journeys: the construction, approve and explicit-run turns of each). Each value is the response body's `assistant_text` field copied byte for byte; `capture_sha256` is the sha256 of the whole captured response file it came from. None of these bodies carried `_answer_shape`. Not hand-written.",
+  "replies": {
+    "pricing-run-complete.explicit-run": {
+      "capture_sha256": "f4022d8b67bd64de645f4c7f707e6d2246acdf344a9af5e57772d9ab3df18188",
+      "exit_path": "agent_lane_v1",
+      "fast_path": "run",
+      "assistant_text": "**This run does not establish whether you should raise Pro to £59**, because it could not test the stated churn limit and did not convert the £20k MRR threshold into the analysis’s outcome frame.\n\n- On the model’s **MRR-only, normalised outcome scale**, the £59-at-release path was most often highest in the simulated comparison (**83.28%** of runs), versus **15.39%** for keeping £49 and **1.33%** for phasing. Those are comparative model results, not probabilities of achieving £20k MRR.\n- The decisive caveat is that **“monthly churn rate < 4%” was unscored**: the model treats churn as calculated from other factors, and the constraint’s frame was unspecified. So this result does not show whether £59 keeps churn below 4%.\n- The comparison also rests on adopted starting assumptions, notably **3% monthly churn**, **250 Pro subscribers**, and **£49/month MRR per Pro subscriber**. The model flags churn, subscriber base, and MRR per subscriber as its strongest structural drivers.\n\nThe next useful reasoning step is to define the churn constraint in a comparable form—whether it is an absolute monthly rate or a change from a measured baseline—so the £59 path can be assessed against the limit rather than on MRR alone."
+    },
+    "hiring-run-blocked.explicit-run": {
+      "capture_sha256": "34916c7a54847d54d143148e098a4ed97477397abb6b369d0442cb75b3d7050b",
